I suppose the point is that they made at least one paying customer
unhappy; a more intense PR effort, if nothing else, might have been
called for.  I'm not clear exactly what happened here, but it looks
like as a precautionary measure, they changed the permissions of
user files without notification -- this could conceivably be a
valid prophylactic tactic if they had some reason to suspect
that user of "cracking".  Should Ray and Harry be pleased at
such attention?

I'd be interested to hear what security hole they were plugging;
sendmail's been kind of tricky security-wise... I remember back
in 1983 or so I was an undergraduate "systems lab" employee in
the CS dept of our local University.  I wrote a little program
that, by cleverly timing the creation of a symbolic link, could
convince sendmail to replace the password file.  Naturally, upon
discovering such a hole we plugged it, and I sent the program
off to the berkeley bugs folks.  Though I'm sure that problem
must have been fixed at some point, I'd think that the security
issues of sendmail would have been thoroughly worked through
by now...

>   They didn't modify your files, they modified their files that they
> are letting you use.

Eh?  Anybody who's selling me system access and doesn't respect
the property rights of _my_ files (yes, mine!) will displease
me.

Fortunately (I guess) internet access is a booming industry and
there are plenty of customers to go around.  In practical terms,
any internet service is only a 'telnet' away from its customers;
there's no reason for anybody doing business on the internet
(as ExI will be, albeit "nonprofit" and "educational") not to
choose a primary site thousands of miles away.  If, say, I were
to create an email listserver for data of some sort (and with
trivial application of public-key crypto such services could
be reliably billed; even simple password schemes could suffice
for FTP-type access), I wouldn't care much if panix or netcom or
joe-schmoe's-internet-access hosted it.  I'd look for price,
reliability, response time, and service.

This whole commercialization of the internet is extremely interesting
to me (and thanks, once again, to Tim May for bringing up the
issue).  I'm curious what's "allowed" right now.  My understanding
(based on rumors, half-remembered usenet posts, inherent bias,
and other non-scientific modes of thought) is that any commercial
activity, without restriction, is allowed on parts of the internet.
The problem is the NSFnet backbone, which has an "acceptable use"
policy (which may never have been formally enforced as far as I
know).  However, I'd heard that this policy was revoked years
ago and that there really isn't even any such policy at all
right now.  Just tremendous peer pressure from the majority of
internet users who were used to having the roads to themselves
at somebody else's expense.

(sign seen on TipperNet on-ramp: "publicly-funded highway:
no commercial traffic allowed!"  note how ridiculous that
would be on interstates...)

derek
