I phrased it sloppily--what you think I meant is what I did mean.
While it's obvious that we can do a great deal more than dogs can, 
I think it's plausible to think that we're missing a great deal of
what's going on, and we may continue to do so, even on the other
side of the Singularity.

This isn't a counsel of despair--we're built to keep learning, and
it's fun.

It's entirely possible that you and I do mean something different
by 'fundamental' and 'understanding'. Also, 'fundamental' is a 
word that you seem to have added (I don't have a copy of my original
post), and it may not be relevent. The issue may simply be how
far it's possible to achieve an understanding of the Universe. 

I don't think it's possible to have a complete understanding even of 
oneself, because if one has a complete representation of oneself
in mind (probably not possible) there wouldn't be anything left
to process it with. 

If (as in Godel's proof) it isn't possible to know all the implications
of the axioms of arithmetic in advance, why would you think that it's
possible to have a complete understanding of the universe?

Maybe part of our disagreement is that you're assuming that we're
talking about a "fundamental" understanding, and I'm thinking of
a "complete" understanding. 

When I talk about a complete understanding, I don't mean that
every little detail is known (though maybe with chaos, you do 
need to know every detail), but that people have accurate
theoretical reasons for thinking that there are no major surprises 
left.

Nancy Lebovitz

