Date: Tue, 9 Nov 93 17:41:22 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Physics as Therapy

[ Comments and questions on _The_Feynman_Lectures_on_Physics_ ]

I took sophomore physics from volumes II and III of these works when I
was an undergraduate at Caltech -- this was about two years after Feynman
himself gave the lectures.  (I placed out of freshman physics, largely
because I had read volume I and worked all the problems in it during the
preceding summer.)  Later I kept them handy as reference during
my PhD program, and reread all three in preparation for my oral PhD
qualifier exam (this was at U. C. Berkeley in the 1970s).  I think they
are wonderful.  Volume III -- on quantum mechanics -- particularly stands
out for a rigorous treatment of the subject with linear algebra as opposed
to differential equations; my experience suggests that most people
encountering quantum mechanics for the first time are inadequately versed
in differential equations to use them fluently; hence the more traditional
treatment, developing quantum mechanics from the Schroedinger equation,
gets bogged down.

Do not neglect the problem books that accompany the three volumes --
perhaps they are bound in with them by now.  These contain a lot of
fascinating physics problems, some (I think) with answers given, some
without.

                                    -- Jay Freeman

